.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1243 AM PDT Sat Oct 3 2026
(Saturday and Sunday)

The 00Z sounding observed an 850 mb temperature of 22.95 degrees
Celsius and a 500 mb height of 5900 meters, both of these are above
the 90th percentile. The heat will peak this weekend as an upper-
level longwave ridge drifts in from the Eastern Pacific Ocean. A
Heat Advisory will go into effect at 11 AM Saturday and run through
10 PM Sunday for most of the Bay Area and Central Coast. It was
extended in area to include the Carmel Valley, Hollister Valley,
Marin Coastal Range, Northern Monterey Bay, Northern Salinas Valley,
and San Francisco. Now, the only places that are not included is the
North Bay Coast, San Francisco Peninsula Coast, and Southern
Monterey Bay; this is banking on a compressed marine layer keeping
these spots cool. If the compressed marine layer is able to survive,
expect reduced visibility from fog along the coast. If it is not
able to survive, coastal conditions will be hotter than currently
forecast. Moderate to major HeatRisk will result from maximum
temperatures 10 to 20 degrees above normal. Minimum temperatures 10
to 20 degrees above normal with weaker than normal onshore flow will
bring limited relief overnight, also contributing to HeatRisk. Even
less relief is expected in the higher terrain where elevated fire
weather conditions continue with hot and dry conditions, prominent
thermal belting, and prolonged burn periods.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1243 AM PDT Sat Oct 3 2026
(Monday through Friday)

The Heat Advisory will remain in effect through 10 PM Monday for the
interior Bay Area and Central Coast. Upper-level shortwave troughing
will undercut the ridge and bring an increase in onshore flow
Monday; however, the interior will remain hot. Global ensemble
clusters are in agreement that heights will rebuild Tuesday and peak
Wednesday. With little change in the day-to-day forecast and the
cumulative heat impacts, we could be looking at an extended Heat
Advisory. This is especially true for the East Bay and South Bay
where major HeatRisk is likely (60%+) through Wednesday. The marine
layer will remain compressed during this time, keeping the coast
cool and likely reducing visibility from fog. Elevated fire weather
conditions will continue in the higher terrain through Thursday for
hot and dry conditions with prominent thermal belting and prolonged
burn periods. Thursday remains uncertain on ridge vs. trough, but by
Friday it is likely that heights will begin to fall.

